<div class="article-body">
    <Fragment set:html={`<h2>Why Use Chalk for Dead Hangs</h2>
        <p>Gym chalk is magnesium carbonate. It dries the skin and increases friction between skin and bar. Use it if sweat, not fatigue, ends your hang. No study of chalk and dead hang time was found. The size of the effect is unknown.</p>
        <p>Moisture builds up between your skin and the bar during a hang, friction drops and your fingers can slide open before your forearms give out. Slipping from sweaty hands is a common reason for ending a hang early. Chalk will not change a hold that ends because your forearms are tired. If that is your limit, read why <a href="/grip-strength/#grip-fails-first">grip gives out first</a>.</p>
        <p>Chalk is one of the cheapest items in the <a href="/equipment-guide/">dead hang equipment</a> list. Check the current price on Amazon before buying.</p>

        <h2>Liquid Chalk</h2>
        <p>Liquid chalk suspends magnesium carbonate in an alcohol solution. Squeeze a small amount onto your palm and rub your hands together. The alcohol evaporates and leaves a dry white coating on the skin.</p>
        <p>One coat often lasts a short session. Reapply if your palms feel damp. Liquid chalk makes less airborne dust than loose block chalk. It is the usual choice for indoor training.</p>

        <div class="callout">
          <h3>Liquid Chalk Quick Facts</h3>
          <ul>
            <li>Best for: home training, indoor gyms, clean environments</li>
            <li>Application: one coat per session; rub hands until dry</li>
            <li>Cleanup: washes off with soap and water</li>
          </ul>
        </div>

        <p>Look for liquid chalk that lists magnesium carbonate first among the ingredients. A thicker formula usually carries more chalk per coat than a watery one.</p>
        <p>Check the current price on Amazon: <a href="https://www.amazon.com/s?k=liquid+chalk+gym&tag=deadhangs-20" rel="nofollow sponsored noopener noreferrer" target="_blank">Browse liquid chalk on Amazon</a>.</p>

        <h2>Block Chalk</h2>
        <p>Block chalk is compressed magnesium carbonate. Break a chunk off the block and rub it between your palms to coat your hands with loose powder. Reapply between sets as needed. Block chalk is usually the cheapest option per gram.</p>
        <p>Block chalk creates visible dust. Fine particles float in the air and settle on nearby surfaces. This is acceptable in garage gyms, outdoor training areas and commercial lifting gyms. Keep it out of bedrooms, living rooms and any space with sensitive electronics or finishes.</p>

        <div class="callout">
          <h3>Block Chalk Quick Facts</h3>
          <ul>
            <li>Best for: garage gyms, outdoor training, climbing walls</li>
            <li>Application: rub between palms before each set</li>
            <li>Cleanup: sweep or vacuum chalk dust from surfaces</li>
          </ul>
        </div>

        <p>Store block chalk in a sealed bag or airtight container. Exposed chalk absorbs moisture from the air and clumps.</p>
        <p>Check the current price on Amazon: <a href="https://www.amazon.com/s?k=block+chalk+gym&tag=deadhangs-20" rel="nofollow sponsored noopener noreferrer" target="_blank">Browse block chalk on Amazon</a>.</p>

        <h2>Chalk Alternatives</h2>
        <p>Several other grip products compete with traditional chalk. Each has a specific use case.</p>

        <h3>Chalk Balls</h3>
        <p>A chalk ball is a mesh bag filled with loose chalk powder. Squeeze the ball to release chalk through the mesh. This controls the amount applied and reduces airborne dust compared to loose block chalk. Chalk balls suit gyms that allow chalk but want less mess. Check the current price on Amazon: <a href="https://www.amazon.com/s?k=chalk+ball&tag=deadhangs-20" rel="nofollow sponsored noopener noreferrer" target="_blank">Browse chalk balls on Amazon</a>.</p>

        <h3>Rosin</h3>
        <p>Rosin is pine tree resin ground into a sticky powder. It adds tackiness rather than dryness. Some people prefer the slight adhesive grip. Rosin suits low-humidity environments where sweat is minimal. In high humidity it can turn gummy.</p>

        <h3>Grip Spray</h3>
        <p>Grip spray is an aerosol that coats the palms with a tacky residue. It is quick to apply, makes little mess and fits in a gym bag. It dries fast. Reapply more often than liquid chalk.</p>

        <h3>Eco Chalk</h3>
        <p>Eco chalk is magnesium carbonate blended with drying agents. It is marketed as lower dust. The manufacturer figures were not independently tested. It suits home gyms where regular chalk is too messy but liquid chalk feels insufficient.</p>

        <h2>What to Avoid</h2>
        <p>Several common products hurt dead hang performance or provide no benefit.</p>

        <h3>Baby Powder (Talcum Powder)</h3>
        <p>Baby powder makes surfaces slippery. A dead hang needs the opposite. Never apply baby powder or any talc-based product to your hands before hanging.</p>

        <h3>Gym Gloves</h3>
        <p>Gloves pad the palm and change how the bar feels. Many people skip them for hanging. Use them if you have an open wound or a torn callus and need to continue a session.</p>

        <h3>Too Much Chalk</h3>
        <p>A thick layer of chalk cakes on the bar and creates a slippery powder layer between your hand and the metal. Apply a thin, even coat. Your palms should look dusted, not plastered. Brush excess chalk off the bar between sets.</p>

        <h2>Which Chalk Fits Your Setup</h2>
        <p>Match your chalk type to your training environment.</p>

        <div class="callout">
          <h3>Setup by Environment</h3>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Home training (bedroom, office or living room):</strong> Liquid chalk. Less dust and one coat per session. Washes off with soap.</li>
            <li><strong>Garage or basement gym:</strong> Block chalk. Cheapest per use and easy to reapply. Dust stays in your training space.</li>
            <li><strong>Commercial gym:</strong> Chalk ball or liquid chalk. Both limit mess. Check your gym's chalk policy first.</li>
            <li><strong>Outdoor training:</strong> Block chalk. Wind disperses dust naturally. No cleanup needed.</li>
            <li><strong>Travel:</strong> Liquid chalk in a small squeeze bottle. Packs flat in a gym bag.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>

        <p>Liquid chalk suits most people who train indoors. Add block chalk later if you build a garage gym or train outdoors regularly.</p>
        <p>Chalk helps with sweat. Technique decides which muscles do the work. The <a href="/how-to-deadhang/">dead hang form guide</a> covers grip positions and setup.</p>

        <h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>

        <div class="faq">
          <h3>Does chalk help with dead hangs?</h3>
          <p>Chalk can raise friction between skin and a bar. Many lifters and climbers use it to delay slipping. No study of dead hang time with and without chalk was found. The size of the effect is unknown. Slipping from sweaty hands is a common reason for ending a hang early.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="faq">
          <h3>Is liquid chalk better than block chalk?</h3>
          <p>Liquid chalk goes on as a coating and makes less airborne dust than loose block chalk. It suits indoor and home use. Block chalk is cheaper per gram and suits garage gyms and outdoor training where dust is not an issue. Both are magnesium carbonate. Brands differ and no head-to-head test was found.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="faq">
          <h3>Can I use gloves instead of chalk for dead hangs?</h3>
          <p>Gloves pad the palm and change how the bar feels. Many people skip them for hanging; use them if you have an open wound. Chalk is the usual option for sweaty hands.</p>
        </div>

